{
  "teamId": 143,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Philadelphia Phillies announced themselves immediately, bursting out of the gate with a pair of Opening Day heroics that set the tone for what would become a 96-win season. Alec Bohm's sharp double on March 27th plated two runs on the very first day of the year, and by early April, Bryce Harper and Trea Turner were launching back-to-back home runs to fuel another statement victory. This was a club brimming with offensive firepower \u2014 212 home runs, a .759 OPS, and 778 runs scored \u2014 and the lineup wasted no time proving it.\n\nThe middle stretch of the season told a more complicated story. The bullpen became a persistent source of anxiety, with Orion Kerkering surrendering crushing home runs in late April and Jordan Romano repeatedly coughing up leads in close games. A brutal three-run inside-the-park home run off Romano on July 8th \u2014 one of the most damaging single plays of the year \u2014 exemplified the roster's Achilles' heel. Yet the offense kept the ship afloat. Kyle Schwarber's grand slam in mid-July, Alec Bohm's clutch extra-base hits, and Trea Turner's knack for rising in big moments ensured Philadelphia stayed comfortably above water through the dog days.\n\nAs summer gave way to fall, the Phillies found a second gear. Trea Turner's walk-off single on August 30th \u2014 driving in three runs and sending Citizens Bank Park into a frenzy \u2014 was the symbolic high-water mark of the regular season. The team posted the wins necessary to secure a playoff berth and entered October as a genuine contender, finishing 96-66 against a 3.79 ERA that ranked among the National League's best.\n\nThe postseason, however, belonged to the Los Angeles Dodgers. In the NLDS, the Phillies ran into a buzzsaw they could not solve. Matt Strahm surrendered a pivotal three-run homer to Teoscar Hern\u00e1ndez in Game 1, and the pattern of costly pitching moments \u2014 so familiar from the regular season \u2014 reprised itself on the biggest stage. A critical fielding error by Orion Kerkering in Game 3 opened the door for the Dodgers to seize control of the series for good. Philadelphia's bats flickered \u2014 Nick Castellanos drove in timely runs and showed fight \u2014 but it was not enough. The 2025 Phillies were a legitimately great regular-season team whose October ended too soon, leaving a talented roster to wonder what might have been.",
  "chapters": [
